WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.834
-I'm so happy you're here.
I always love seeing you.

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:05.133
We have a lot to get into.
A lot of good news for you.

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:08.968
But last time I saw you,
we both played --

00:00:08.968 --> 00:00:11.434
Justin Timberlake
does the 8AM Tournament

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:13.200
for charity in Las Vegas.
-Yes.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.067
-And it was really, really fun.
-Yeah, it's a blast.

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.067
-Yeah.
-It's a great, great time.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:20.267
I mean...
-The winners were crowned,

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:22.501
and I don't know.
-I don't remember. Who won?

00:00:22.501 --> 00:00:24.934
-Oh.
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:26.968
-Me and Justin Timberlake.
-The people whose name is on --

00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:28.801
[ Laughter ]

00:00:28.801 --> 00:00:30.033
The people whose name
is on the tournament.

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:31.267
-We won the tournament.
-I don't know. Shocking.

00:00:31.267 --> 00:00:32.567
-What are you talking about?
-I don't know.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:33.934
-We just happened to win.
-Guys --

00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:36.167
"Hey, J.J., will you come play
in my golf tournament?"

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:38.767
"Yeah. Okay. It's gonna be
a great competition. Sure."

00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:41.167
"I won. Congrats."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.634
"This is fun."

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.300
-Dude, we have fun.
We --

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.367
-You contributed a lot.
I mean, in --

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:50.133
by way of outfit,
by way of outfit, not golf.

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:51.501
Jimmy --
Guys, I have to --

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:54.868
Jimmy has the best outfits
at this event every single year.

00:00:54.868 --> 00:00:57.901
I just have one problem
with them.

00:00:57.901 --> 00:01:01.267
They are the hottest outfits --
like, temperature-wise.

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:04.467
Not -- Jimmy's a great,
great-looking man.

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.100
-Thank you for saying that.
-Great-looking man.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.167
-But he's wearing full cotton
in Las Vegas in April.

00:01:10.167 --> 00:01:12.634
-I always forget how hot
it is in Vegas, but here's you

00:01:12.634 --> 00:01:14.367
next to me.
-Yes.

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:15.701
-I'm wearing, like -- Yeah.

00:01:15.701 --> 00:01:18.934
-Sweat-wicking Polo.
-Yeah.

00:01:18.934 --> 00:01:20.434
-What you wear to play golf.
-Yeah.

00:01:20.434 --> 00:01:22.467
-Jimmy's got a cardigan.

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:24.501
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:26.734
-Yeah. I'm dressed
like the Golden Bachelor.

00:01:26.734 --> 00:01:27.834
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:31.334
I -- I forgot how hot
it is in Vegas.

00:01:31.334 --> 00:01:32.968
And, you know,
but I don't even think about it

00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:34.801
because we're always goofing off
and doing stuff

00:01:34.801 --> 00:01:37.133
and just having fun.
I wanted to show --

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:39.467
Speaking of having fun,
this is a great picture

00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:41.601
I have of you from --
from the tournament.

00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.167
-Oh, God.
[ Laughs ]

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:46.067
-What, uh...?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:47.467
What was happening?

00:01:47.467 --> 00:01:49.634
-Jimmy, I told my -- I told
my grandma to watch tonight.

00:01:49.634 --> 00:01:51.834
Now I got a ass shot.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.834 --> 00:01:54.801
-Did you --
Did you fall in the bunker?

00:01:54.801 --> 00:01:56.801
-So, what happened...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.801 --> 00:01:58.300
-So, what happened?
What happened?

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.868
-What had happened was,

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:02.601
my ball went in the bunker.
It was a scramble.

00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:03.968
Obviously, we're not
going to use it.

00:02:03.968 --> 00:02:06.601
So I went to retrieve my ball
from the bunker.

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:08.100
I just wanted to grab it.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.634
I grabbed it and realized
there's no possible way for me

00:02:11.634 --> 00:02:15.701
to get back out of the bunker.
So I tried to crawl backwards.

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:17.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:19.100
-What?
-It didn't work.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.567
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:23.501
So, to my savior,

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:27.801
J.R. Smith came and grabbed me
and pulled me out.

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.133
-That's hilarious.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.200
-I don't even know where in life
I was at that moment

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.167
when I was facedown
in a bunker in Las Vegas,

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:36.467
and J.R. Smith said,

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:38.033
"Hey, brother, just let me
give you a hand."

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.868
I was like, "All right."

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:41.801
-It was so fun.
-Yeah.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:44.133
Since then, I gotta say,
congratulations.

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:46.367
You have a baby.
You have a 1-year-old baby.

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:49.234
-I do. Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:49.234 --> 00:02:52.033
-Congratulations, bud.

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:53.767
What's it like?
What's he like?

00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:55.067
-He's the best.
He's the best.

00:02:55.067 --> 00:02:56.300
He's crawling
all over the place.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.501
He's scratching my face,
which is a little tough,

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:02.234
but it's truly the biggest
joy in the entire world.

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:04.133
I mean, nobody can explain it.

00:03:04.133 --> 00:03:05.501
Obviously,
parents tried to tell me

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.167
all the time what it's like
once you have a child.

00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:10.033
But until you have it,
you just have no idea the joy

00:03:10.033 --> 00:03:12.267
and the love and the happiness
that it brings, so...

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:14.167
We were reading your book
last week, so thank you.

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:15.567
Yes, we were,
we were reading your book.

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:18.334
-And he got to see you play,
too, right?

00:03:18.334 --> 00:03:20.200
-He did.
Last weekend in Houston,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.467
they did a great job
honoring me.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:24.300
He got to watch me play.
We got to watch my brother play.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.400
It was my mom's birthday.
It was a very,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.701
very special weekend all around.
-How did that feel?

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:30.334
Look at --
Look at the crowd.

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:33.067
Everyone's got your number
and your name there.

00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:34.934
I mean, they love you there,
buddy.

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.133
-So, when I first got drafted
to Houston,

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.033
on Draft Night,
they booed me.

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:40.767
They booed me hard.
Really hard.

00:03:40.767 --> 00:03:42.367
-Really?
-They didn't want me.

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:43.868
So I looked at it
as a challenge,

00:03:43.868 --> 00:03:45.534
and I looked at it as something
that I wanted

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:47.667
to prove them that I was worthy.

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:50.100
-Yeah.
-So, now, 12 years later,

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.968
to be able to leave the stadium
with that scene

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:55.033
was was pretty cool.
It was special.

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:58.167
-And it was Houston versus --
-Houston versus my brother.

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:00.701
So I was sitting in the suite
thinking, like,

00:04:00.701 --> 00:04:03.367
"Okay.
Everybody's wearing my jersey.

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:06.467
My brother's on the other team.
He made a play.

00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:08.667
Do I cheer? Do I...?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:10.334
I had no idea what to do.

00:04:10.334 --> 00:04:11.934
-You don't want
to become a meme?

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:14.100
-Yeah, I have no idea.
-Like a zombie.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.801
-And he's like...
-Yeah, like he's --

00:04:15.801 --> 00:04:17.467
My brain broke.
I was in a blender.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:19.267
I was in a --
I was in a blender, Jimmy.

00:04:19.267 --> 00:04:20.300
I was literally
standing up there, like,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.467
"I don't know what to do."

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:23.667
-Here's you and your brother
and the baby.

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:25.701
-Come on.
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:04:25.701 --> 00:04:28.067
-Is this the coolest thing?
This is the greatest.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.968
-That's going to be in a very,
very large frame

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:31.868
in our house for a long time.
-Yeah.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:33.300
Please tell your brother
I said hi, too.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.834
-I will. Thank you.
-Now, can you explain this?

00:04:35.834 --> 00:04:37.100
'Cause I go, "Oh, my God."

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.701
You were a great
host on "Saturday Night Live."

00:04:38.701 --> 00:04:41.667
And you just got the knack.
You have the charm.

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:43.501
You just got it all.
People love you.

00:04:43.501 --> 00:04:46.300
I go, "He's going to retire
when he does."

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:47.701
I don't even know
who you're going to retire.

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:50.267
But I go When he does,
he's going to do movies.

00:04:50.267 --> 00:04:52.901
He's going to do something.

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:55.267
-I want your job.
I'm waiting.

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:57.334
[ Laughter ]

00:04:57.334 --> 00:04:58.968
I'm waiting for your job.

00:04:58.968 --> 00:05:00.434
-What are you talking about?

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:03.567
-I would really like to work
with Quest, the boys, the crowd.

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:05.934
I'd like to walk out to a
standing -- yeah.

00:05:05.934 --> 00:05:07.334
Thank you.
See?

00:05:07.334 --> 00:05:08.667
[ Theme music plays ]

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:11.467
-No, don't play it!
No, don't play it!

00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:13.400
-Welcome to
"The J.J. Watt Tonight Show"!

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.534
-That's not what you --
-Not at all happening.

00:05:16.534 --> 00:05:19.300
-No, I mean, you get to walk
out to a standing ovation

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.434
every single night.
You get to have this --

00:05:21.434 --> 00:05:23.901
One of the things --
You ask about movies and TV.

00:05:23.901 --> 00:05:25.801
The thing that's the truth
about it is,

00:05:25.801 --> 00:05:27.834
I came from a sport where,
if you make a play,

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:30.100
if you get a sack,
there's this energy.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:31.901
The crowd gets into it,
there's cheers.

00:05:31.901 --> 00:05:34.100
There's something
about that adrenaline.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.434
You do movies,

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:37.934
you crack a joke
or you say the perfect line,

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:40.734
and it is crickets.
I do not like that.

00:05:40.734 --> 00:05:42.901
-You can't laugh
because it's a movie.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:45.467
-Yeah, you can't, like --
your co-workers aren't laughing.

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:47.701
I like opening the curtain

00:05:47.701 --> 00:05:49.868
and it's a loud
standing ovation.

00:05:49.868 --> 00:05:51.200
So I don't know how much you pay

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.000
these people every night,
but I am --

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:55.467
I am a big,
big fan of this setting.

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:59.200
-Yeah, but you ended up doing
a move that I didn't see coming.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.634
You own a soccer team.
-Yes, football.

00:06:02.634 --> 00:06:04.434
You call it soccer, I might get
kicked out of England.

00:06:04.434 --> 00:06:05.434
-Sorry. Football?

00:06:05.434 --> 00:06:07.701
Yes, football.
-Football.

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:09.801
Was this --
Your wife, Obviously. -Is it?

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:12.334
She's a professional soccer --
football player.

00:06:12.334 --> 00:06:14.868
Oh, geez. I'm screwed.
Don't send this across.

00:06:14.868 --> 00:06:16.133
Don't send it!
-You just said it!

00:06:16.133 --> 00:06:17.167
I didn't know --
You just said it, yeah.

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:18.701
-It's over.
-Yeah. So...?

00:06:18.701 --> 00:06:20.834
-But, yeah. So we've wanted to
do this for a few years,

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.801
and we were trying to find
the right club,

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:23.934
and we found Burnley.

00:06:23.934 --> 00:06:26.200
We went over there,
we visited the town.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.667
It's a very small, hardworking
town with great people

00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:30.167
that just live and die
with their club.

00:06:30.167 --> 00:06:31.968
And we really,
really love Burnley.

00:06:31.968 --> 00:06:33.267
-It's a great Burnley
is Premier.

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.234
Premier League.
-Premier League.

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:35.801
-Yes, Premier --
Premier League.

00:06:35.801 --> 00:06:37.434
I'm learning all sorts
of pronunciations.

00:06:37.434 --> 00:06:39.601
-Premier League.
-I have to drink tea now.

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:41.400
It's a whole thing.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.834
-Yeah. Explain to me --
this is something the team does.

00:06:43.834 --> 00:06:45.801
You brought me --
-I did.

00:06:45.801 --> 00:06:48.267
-A treat.
-Yeah. So every game in Burnley,

00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:50.300
we have a little thing
called a Benny &amp; Hot.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.934
So, back -- Long story,
but back during World War II,

00:06:52.934 --> 00:06:54.334
the soldiers came
back to England,

00:06:54.334 --> 00:06:57.267
and one of the things that
kept them warm in the trenches

00:06:57.267 --> 00:06:59.868
was Benedictine and hot water.
They just put it together.

00:06:59.868 --> 00:07:02.901
So now at Burnley,
at our matches, when it's cold

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:04.667
and we need something
to warm us up,

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:06.434
we drink Benedictine
and hot water.

00:07:06.434 --> 00:07:09.934
So it's -- it's --
Jimmy, I don't know

00:07:09.934 --> 00:07:12.234
if you're aware
of the term "acquired taste."

00:07:12.234 --> 00:07:14.701
[ Laughter ]

00:07:14.701 --> 00:07:15.968
-No, I --
-It's an acquired taste.

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:17.367
-It looks fantastic.
Look how --

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:18.901
-It's not, it's not,
no, it's not.

00:07:18.901 --> 00:07:20.968
-This is gonna fantastic.
This is for Burnley.

00:07:20.968 --> 00:07:23.200
-So, we say, "Up the clarets."

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:24.934
We have a big match tomorrow
against Chelsea.

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:26.734
-It is hot.
-So we say, "Up the Clarets."

00:07:26.734 --> 00:07:28.934
-Up the Clarets.
-Cheers to Burnley.

00:07:28.934 --> 00:07:30.067
-To Burnley.

00:07:30.067 --> 00:07:31.067
♪♪

00:07:31.067 --> 00:07:32.067
-[ Coughs, spits ]

00:07:32.067 --> 00:07:34.100
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:36.400
-It's -- It's so good.
It's so good.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.767
You guys should all try it.
It's delicious.

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:42.200
-Oh, my God.
-I don't...

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:45.033
-Oh, my God.
What does it taste like?

00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:46.267
-Like -- Like, uh...

00:07:46.267 --> 00:07:48.801
-Like a melted cough drop
or something?

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:51.601
-Yeah. That's dead on!
-It's like a licorice.

00:07:51.601 --> 00:07:53.901
-It's like black licorice
and cough syrup.

00:07:53.901 --> 00:07:55.767
-Whoo!
-It's just so good.

00:07:55.767 --> 00:07:57.400
I just don't know
how it hasn't caught on

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:58.701
in the rest of the world.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:58.701 --> 00:08:01.400
-Well, I just wanted to say,
you're my best friend.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:02.934
-Yeah, it's great.
It's great.

00:08:02.934 --> 00:08:04.834
-And you're the best friend
I ever had.

00:08:04.834 --> 00:08:06.467
-Does this thing do karaoke?
-You're the best friend

00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:09.868
I ever had.
-♪ Sweet Caroline ♪

00:08:09.868 --> 00:08:12.467
-♪ Living in a lonely world ♪

00:08:12.467 --> 00:08:14.767
J.J. Watt, everybody.

00:08:14.767 --> 00:08:17.501
J.J. and I are doing
something fun after the break!

00:08:17.501 --> 00:08:18.801
Stick around, everybody!

